6.m:
Thus in the past, Kāma together with his arrows and bow was burnt by Śambhu, the foremost among the gods;
thenceforth the great archer, celebrated as the ‘Bodiless One’ by the gods, was worshiped by the leaders of the immortals.

Thus ends the sixth chapter in the glorious Vāmanapurāṇa.

7.m:
Cyavana said:
‘On earth, Naimiṣa is the tīrtha; in the sky, it is Puṣkara;
and in the depths of Rasātala, O mighty-armed one, they know Cakra-tīrtha.’

Prahlāda said:
‘Arise! We shall go to bathe in the Naimiṣa tīrtha;
we shall behold Acyuta, the lotus-eyed one clothed in yellow!’

11.m:
Saṃdaṃśa and Lohapiṇḍa, and likewise Karambhasikatā;
and another, the terrible Kṣāranadī, and another, Kṛmibhojana;
and the eighteenth is declared the terrible Vaitaraṇī river.
